--TEST--
Every condition refuses a call that returns `void` where it is written, and the operators beside them keep their own code — `rule:expressions/truthy-table`, `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:expressions/truthy-positions` makes a condition the one place a value is tested without `as`,
// and its truthy table has a row for every type there is — which is why a
// call that returns `void` shows up here as nothing at all rather than as a
// mismatch. Unrefused, each line below reached `nvs-ir`'s truthy slice with
// no value to convert and failed the whole compilation naming a compiler bug.
//
// This sweeps one spelling per position, in source order, so a position that
// stopped refusing shows up as a missing line *and* as a shifted `&&` below
// rather than as a plausible answer.
class Effect {
    public static function run(): void {}
}

// The four statement conditions, which go through the one site that asks what
// a condition's type is (`nvs_types::expr::check_condition`).
if (Effect::run()) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}
while (Effect::run()) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}
do {
    echo "unreachable\n";
} while (Effect::run());
int $i = 0;
for (; Effect::run(); $i = $i + 1) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}

// A ternary's condition, and the elvis spelling that tests its own value.
mixed $ternary = Effect::run() ? "a" : "b";
mixed $elvis = Effect::run() ?: "b";

// `!` and `empty()` are that same truthy test written out, so they take the
// condition's code rather than one of `rule:types/arithmetic`'s operand ones.
bool $not = !Effect::run();
bool $voidIsEmpty = empty(Effect::run());

// The line the split is pinned by: `&&` is an *operand* position of `rule:types/arithmetic`
// 's table, so it keeps `E0718` however much it reads like a condition.
// One rule, one code, both ways round —
// `nvs_diagnostics::code::E_VOID_IS_NOT_A_CONDITION` is that split's home.
mixed $and = Effect::run() && $not;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
